# Context

## Background
Migration 009 builds an index over events: 3 min 41 s on a copy of the
hub with 2.9 million events. It holds the write lock for that time.
Before this change, store.Open gave up after one busy timeout (5 s).
ohya serve and the ingest service then exited, and a new ohya mcp
process ended before its first tool call.

## Why This Way
- An up-to-date database needs no write lock to open. Measured: a
  bounded open under a held BEGIN IMMEDIATE succeeded at once. Only a
  pending migration, or a WAL recovery conflict between two processes
  that open at once, waits. So the wait is an option for processes that
  must survive a migration, not the default. One-shot commands and
  ohya setup stay bounded, and their error now names the cause.
- ohya mcp completes its handshake before the open, in a goroutine.
  Claude Code does not retry the first connection of a stdio server;
  the user has to reconnect it by hand. A server that answers with
  "still opening" keeps that session usable after the migration.
- report runs at most once per busy timeout (atMostEvery), so a log
  gets one line per 5 s and not one line per 20 ms attempt.

## Gotchas
- A context cannot shorten one attempt: SQLite runs its busy handler
  without a cancel. A process that stops during the wait exits up to
  5 s later. Claude Code stops a stdio server with SIGINT, SIGTERM
  after 100 ms, then SIGKILL after 400 ms more, so it kills such a
  process. That is safe: the open holds no transaction, and a
  migration is atomic. Source: code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp, read
  2026-11-25.
- The command tests hold the database with a connection in
  locking_mode=EXCLUSIVE, which blocks each statement of another
  connection. Only a close releases that lock; a rollback does not.
- Mutation checks (ten): each reverted part failed its test: the
  option flag, the report gate, the wait limit, the context check, the
  5 s message, atMostEvery, the Opening deadline, the open error
  exit, and the WaitForLock option of each service.

